The code statements that lead to the bug are:
f2fs_update_read_extent_cache_range(dn, start, 0, index - start);
f2fs_update_age_extent_cache_range(dn, start, index - start);
Reason:
When `index` equals `start` (e.g., if the loop in `f2fs_do_zero_range` does not execute or breaks before incrementing `index`), the length `index - start` becomes zero. This results in an attempt to update the extent cache with a zero-length extent, which triggers a BUG_ON in `__update_extent_tree_range`.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (index > start) {
    f2fs_update_read_extent_cache_range(dn, start, 0, index - start);
    f2fs_update_age_extent_cache_range(dn, start, index - start);
}
Reason:
Adding a condition `if (index > start)` ensures the extent cache is only updated when the length `index - start` is positive, preventing the zero-sized extent update that caused the kernel BUG. This guards both against the initial case where `start == end` and against early loop termination due to errors.

